  "title": {
    "en": "Seaduck, Shag and Cormorant"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Eider, Common, Velvet and Surf Scoter, Long-tailed Duck, King Eider, Shag and Cormorant"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Offshore ducks gather in rafts on the sea, and in winter they are a big part of every seawatch from the east coast of Britain to Denmark. Most are dark, far away and in poor light, so the group works with silhouettes: the size of the raft, the shape of the head, whether there is white on the head or wing.\n\nThe big numbers come from the Baltic and the Arctic: hundreds of thousands of Eiders and scoters winter in Danish and German waters, in the Wadden Sea and off the east coast of Scotland. Common Scoters pass in huge flocks in spring and autumn. A Velvet Scoter or a Surf Scoter is a matter of scanning a raft for one with a white head patch or wing patch.\n\nIn the same view you usually find a Cormorant or a Shag sitting on a post or a rock. They are not ducks but they belong to the same coastal list: the Shag is smaller, slimmer, and has a thinner neck and bill."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "raft"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A flock of ducks sitting together on the sea."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "wing patch"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A patch of white on the wing. A Velvet Scoter shows a white wing patch when it flaps, and a Common Scoter does not."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "bill knob"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A lump at the base of the bill. A male Common Scoter has a yellow-orange knob."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "crest"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A tuft of feathers on the head. Shags grow a curly crest in spring."
      }
    }
  ],
